            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed through electrical outlets on your exterior walls, the weather stripping along the exterior doors,the dryer exhaust penetrations,the air registers, the pellet stove flue penetrations and around the attic access hatch. 



I recommend to outfit electrical outlets located along the exterior walls with an electrical wall plate gasket. 



I recommend replacing the weather stripping along all of your exterior doors. Using a screw in aluminum style weather stripping may help to create a better seal, as well as using an adjustable strike plate to ensure the door makes good contact with the weather stripping while closed. 



I recommend using high temperature silicone sealant to seal up the flue penetration of the pellet stove.



I recommend using cualking to seal the dryer exhaust penetration, in the event that this does not create a sufficient air seal, you could consider flashing fixtures for the penetrations.



The attic access hatch had quite a bit of air leakage entering from the attic space. I recommend using a removable caulking to seal up the access hatch and also recommend rearranging the electrical wiring in a manner that allows the access hatch to form a complete seal. 



The air registers on the floor at various parts of the house were observed to have substantial air leakage,upon inspection of the crawlspace, there are strong reasons to believe that the air barrier between the crawlspace and the floor above may not be continuous, this could account for a majority of the air leakage observed from the registers due to air making its way through the openings intended to keep the floor insulation warm and could be the cause of the cold floors in the winter.



I recommend using acoustical cualking to seal the edges of the air barrier on crawlspace fixtures which I assume contain plumbing and ductwork. I also recommend consulting specialists that could perform an indepth investigation into the integrity of the air barrier in the crawlspace.



Savings: For a minimum 26% improvement you could potentially save $ 480 per year in energy costs. There are currently no rebates available for air sealing improvements from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $715 and $1,300 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved. This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake. 

An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional. 

</airSeal>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 181 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly.

When it is time to replace your water system, I recommend that you replace your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.95 or higher.



This may increase the overall energy consumption of the home but would reduce the cost associated with the hot water system due to propane being a cheaper alternative to electric water heating.



Savings:

By replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.95, you could potentially save $ 1000 per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program 

</HotWaterSystem>
                <Renewables>

Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely. The short and dark days of winter provide little or no usable energy, depending on the latitude in the NWT. However, the long hours of sunlight in spring and summer can provide usable heat and electricity for up to eight months of the year.  As prices continue to drop, solar PV panels are becoming competitive with the cost of diesel generation. 



Savings:

By installing a 4 kW solar photovoltaic system, you could potentially save $ 1300  per year in energy costs.

Currently there are no rebates available from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program for electricity generation systems installed in a Hydro Community, including Yellowknife.



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,300 per KW is available for installing a photovoltaic solar panel. If you choose to install a photovoltaic (PV) system, it is strongly recommended that a full assessment by a professional solar photovoltaic installer, including the measuring of solar irradiance (how much sunlight falls on the roof) is undertaken to provide detailed information on considerations for your home and specific installation recommendations, including the size and related energy production of the system. The rated PV panel must have peak power capacity higher than or equal to 1.0 kW. The system must be composed of photovoltaic (PV) panel and inverter certified to CSA Standards.

</Renewables>
                <AdditionalComments>

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT 

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020. 

Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines. 



ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION 

For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit. 

Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include: 

1. In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report 

2. If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant. 

3. Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit 

4. All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional 

5. It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that: 

- installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional* 

- an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards 

- the heat pump has been sized to your entire home *When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory. 

6. All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada. 



ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES 

You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure. 

Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights Â Hydro Community

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.

Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test = Pass

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (kitchen range hood, bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 1.1 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace and your wood stove vent up a chimney and are susceptible to back drafting.

During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



Ventilation

Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

        - a 300 CFM range hood   

        - two bathroom fans

        - a clothes dryer



You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Fuel Costs - Yellowknife

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

The following Dec. 1, 2022 values, excluding GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:



Electricity - $18.13 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil - $1.24 per liter 

Propane - $0.75 per liter delivered

Wood - $410 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.79 per bag, $351.29 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Dharshan Maheswaran, Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: dharshan.maheswaran@aea.nt.ca

Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
